window.PULSE_CURRENT = {"asOf":"2026-11-25T05:40:00Z","calendar":[{"date":"2026-11-23","event":"CFDT’s two-week deadline for branch wage negotiations after the 9 Nov social conference, with a progress review and results sought by early December","why":"A failed review would bring the next union mobilisation into the Senate’s finance bill stage"},{"date":"2026-11-24","event":"Printed: Ifo 90.6 (90.5 expected, October revised to 90.1; current 89.4, expectations 91.9); US Conference Board confidence 103.3 (92.2 expected); STOXX 600 −0.66% to 632.74","why":"The Ifo confirms German momentum but manufacturing slipped; the STOXX 600 is back under the 635 trigger."},{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"ECB non-policy Governing Council meeting: reported discussion of raising the minimum reserve ratio from 1% to 2% (decision may slip to 17 Dec); US core PCE and preliminary Q3 GDP (US markets closed 26 Nov, early close 27 Nov)","why":"A liquidity-tightening step alongside the December hike; the US inflation print sets the Fed leg for 9 Dec"},{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"Italian Treasury auctions: BTP Short Term and BTP€i (BOT follows 26 Nov, medium and long-term BTPs 27 Nov)","why":"First Italian supply after the spread widened to 107 bp; demand confirms or questions the calm periphery"},{"date":"2026-11-25","event":"Trump meets NATO’s Rutte in Palm Beach (late evening) on Ukraine talks and the European role; US PCE and Thanksgiving-week data before the 26 Nov holiday","why":"Whether Europe gets a seat in the Ukraine, Russia, Europe, US format Merz described; US markets shut 26 Nov and close early 27 Nov"},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"European Council: Costa begins consultations on the ECB presidency (de Cos, Knot) and board seats (Brunnermeier; Portugal weighing a candidate) with Parliament and the Governing Council","why":"Sets the ECB’s 2027 leadership before Lagarde leaves on 7 Feb"},{"date":"2026-11-26","event":"ECB account of the 29 Oct meeting; constitutional deadline for the French social security budget; GfK consumer confidence, Germany; EU leaders meet in Brussels on 26-27 Nov expected to endorse the ECB leadership package (Hernández de Cos as president, Brunnermeier as chief economist, a French candidate for Schnabel’s seat)","why":"The account shows how close the Council came to hiking in October; after the deadline the French government can use ordinances for that text; leaders endorse the succession after the 18 Nov Eurogroup nominations, with final approval expected in December"},{"date":"2026-11-27","event":"EC economic sentiment and final consumer confidence, November (ESI prior 96.9; Trading Economics forecast 96.1; flash confidence −16.9); German unemployment, November (prior 6.4%); French and Spanish preliminary November inflation (Trading Economics week-ahead; France at its highest since December 2023, Spain since February 2023); Schnabel’s Executive Board seat (she leaves 3 Jan 2027; Laurence Boone seen as the likely French nominee)","why":"Last sentiment data before the December ECB projections"},{"date":"2026-11-28","event":"French Senate public vote on the first (revenue) part of the 2027 finance bill (may run into 29 Nov)","why":"Decides whether the Senate text reaches the CMP with a revenue side the Assemblée rejected on 4 Nov"},{"date":"2026-11-29","event":"Spanish general election, called by Sánchez on 5 Oct after parliament rejected his housing measures; polls point to a PP-led coalition with Vox","why":"Spain is the bloc’s growth engine; a PP-Vox government would be the first with the far right since 1975 and changes the fiscal and energy stance"},{"date":"2026-11-30","event":"German CPI flash, November; German retail sales, October (prior −2.0% m/m); first session after the Spanish election","why":"The German flash leads the euro area print by a day"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Euro area flash HICP, November (Trading Economics calendar; earlier listed here for 2 Dec; Pantheon 4.1%); final manufacturing PMI; euro area unemployment for October follows the same week","why":"The last inflation print before the 16-17 Dec ECB meeting; unemployment is 6.5% after upward revisions"},{"date":"2026-12-01","event":"Euro area final manufacturing PMI, November (flash 52.9)","why":"Confirms or revises the two-month high"},{"date":"2026-12-03","event":"Euro area final composite and services PMIs, November; euro area unemployment, October (prior 6.5%); euro area producer prices, October (prior +11.1% y/y)","why":"Final confirmation of the November flash and the last labour print before the ECB meeting"},{"date":"2026-12-07","event":"German factory orders, October (prior +4.4% m/m, +3.3% y/y); German industrial production follows on 8 Dec","why":"The German swing economy after the September rebound"},{"date":"2026-12-09","event":"Fed decision (8-9 Dec; 90% to 91.5% priced for 25 bp after Cook and Paulson; another expected in January); French Senate final vote on the 2027 finance bill (16:30)","why":"Sets the dollar leg of EUR/USD below 1.10 and the global curve the Bund follows"},{"date":"2026-12-11","event":"Lecornu’s follow-up meeting with the civil-service unions after the professional elections, with first pay measures promised by mid-December","why":"Decides whether the 17 Nov strike becomes a December movement during the finance bill"},{"date":"2026-12-14","event":"Euro area industrial production, October (prior +0.2% m/m, −1.5% y/y)","why":"Whether the September monthly gain holds against a −1.5% annual rate"},{"date":"2026-12-16","event":"UK CPI, November (prior 3.6%, core 2.8%, services 3.7%); UK labour market report","why":"The last UK inflation print before the 17 Dec BoE decision, with December about 80% priced"},{"date":"2026-12-17","event":"ECB decision and projections (16-17 Dec; 91% priced for 25 bp to 2.75%, at least two more priced for 2027); BoE decision (about 80% priced, at least three more by end-2027); French constitutional 70-day limit for the finance bill","why":"The December hike is the base case for both; the projections set the 2027 path and the second hike"},{"date":"2026-12-18","event":"ECB wage tracker update; BoJ decision (79% priced for a hike)","why":"The tracker is the main evidence against a wage-price loop; the BoJ leg sets USD/JPY after the yen’s safe-haven bid"}],"cb":{"label":"Tightening","note":"The ECB held at 2.50% on 29 Oct and a 25 bp hike to 2.75% on 17 Dec is the base case: Sleijpen said on 24 Nov the hike is likely and the direction clear but the path after unclear, and Simkus that more tightening will be needed in 2027 if inflation persists; Reuters says markets see one or two more hikes in 2027. The Ifo at 90.6 and TTF at €91.25 support the hawks, and the Council discusses doubling the minimum reserve ratio on 25 Nov. The BoE is at 4.00% after the 5 Nov hike and Greene backed another rise on 24 Nov while saying a 50 bp move would need clear evidence of second-round effects. Markets price a Fed hike on 9 Dec."},"cbName":"ECB stance","conflicts":[{"a":"The STOXX 600 rose 1.81% on 23 Nov in its best day since May and Nvidia 31.5% after Jensen Huang was restored as CEO","b":"On 24 Nov the STOXX 600 fell 0.66% to 632.74, back under the 635 trigger, chipmakers gave back 6% to 16%, Nvidia fell 5.86%, the semiconductor index 7.9% and the VIX stayed near 26.4","read":"The bounce was short-covering in the names that fell hardest, not a change in the AI-capex worries behind the sell-off. Under the framework the posture stays Risk-off until both restore conditions hold at a close.","trigger":"A STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25 restores Neutral; a BTP spread above 150 bp, an OAT spread above 180 bp or iTraxx Crossover above 400 bp makes the Risk-off a credit one."},{"a":"The Ifo rose to 90.6, the highest since May 2023, Germany’s flash composite PMI is 55.0, a 56-month high, and euro area new orders rose for a fifth month","b":"Ifo manufacturing fell to −1.2 from +0.9, Fuest says the recovery lacks breadth and rests on public spending, France’s composite is 46.2 with a Q4 recession in view and US tariff reports hit German exporters and logistics","read":"Germany is leading on fiscal and defence spending while France contracts; the euro area composite of 51.7 stays under the 52 that reopens Expansion, so the regime stays Slowdown.","trigger":"A final composite or December flash above 52 reopens Expansion; below 51, or a French composite under 45, deepens the Slowdown."},{"a":"Simkus says 25 bp in December may not be enough, sees early second-round effects and a wage tracker moving up, German producer prices rose 8.1% y/y and TTF spiked to €96 on cold and 69.2% storage","b":"Flash PMI selling prices rose at the weakest pace since August with input costs at a three-month low, Brent fell to a three-month low of $89.37 on the IEA surplus, negotiated wages are 2.57% and the French downturn is deflationary","read":"Pipeline and gas pressure argue for the ECB to keep going; survey prices, oil and France argue the December hike is the last. Both sides price December; the argument is 2027, where Reuters sees one or two hikes priced and Trading Economics two by March.","trigger":"A November flash HICP above 4.0% on 1 Dec or TTF above €95 keeps 2027 hikes priced; HICP under 3.8% with TTF under €80 prices them out."},{"a":"The OAT fell 8 bp to 4.79% and the OAT-Bund spread narrowed to about 151 bp after Macron floated a constitutional golden rule, the BTP spread closed 106 bp and banks rose","b":"The golden rule is a January idea needing a three-fifths majority in a parliament that rejected the budget’s revenue part, France expects a 5.4% deficit with debt near 120% of GDP, the Senate vote is 28 Nov with the 17 Dec limit, and the spread is still at the 150 bp warn line","read":"Markets paid for the signal of fiscal intent; delivery depends on Le Pen’s version and on the 2027 budget passing first. France stays the only large euro sovereign at its warn line.","trigger":"An OAT-Bund spread above 180 bp makes the Risk-off a sovereign one; under 140 bp with the budget passed removes France from the warn list."},{"a":"TTF rose 1.69% to €91.25 on cold, weak wind and storage below 69%, and Iran’s army chief ordered Hormuz kept closed until Iran’s seven conditions are met","b":"Qatar’s prime minister says Doha is working to revive US-Iran talks, Brent traded near $86 to $87 in the European morning near three-month lows, and EUR/USD recovered to 1.0961","read":"Gas is a European weather and storage story, oil a global surplus story; a firmer euro trims the imported part. For HICP the gas leg matters more in winter, so the inflation score holds near its low.","trigger":"TTF above €95 at a close with EUR/USD under 1.08 lifts the inflation risk; TTF under €80 lowers the ECB path."}],"drivers":[{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Monday’s rebound was short-covering; the posture stays Risk-off with the index 7 points below the 640 restore line.","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener; finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; Trading Economics","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 −0.66% to 632.74, back under the 635 trigger, as the chip rally fades: Aixtron −16.3% (UBS sell), STMicro −10.2%, Infineon −9.7%, ASML −5.6%; miners almost −5% (Aurubis −15%, Antofagasta −8.1%); Sanofi +9.8% on its $41B Insmed deal, health care +2%. Euro Stoxx 50 −1.16% to 6,003.76, DAX −1.31% to 25,005.51, CAC 40 −0.05% to 8,040.49, FTSE 100 −0.13% to 10,467.07, IBEX −0.86%, FTSE MIB −1.84%.","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/european-shares-end-lower-as-tech-rally-fades-sanofi-gains-ce7851dbdd89f025"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Backs Germany’s 55.0 flash PMI and the ECB hawks, but the manufacturing setback and narrow base keep the regime in Slowdown.","src":"ActionForex; Reuters via RTE; Kotak Neo/Reuters","tag":"Data","title":"Ifo business climate 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 (90.5 expected), the highest since May 2023; current conditions 89.4, expectations 91.9; services +2.4, manufacturing −1.2 from +0.9. Fuest: recovery proceeding but lacks breadth. US Conference Board confidence 103.3 from 91.9 (92.2 expected).","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661933-germany-ifo-business-climate-rises-to-90-6-but-manufacturing-recovery-loses-momentum/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Keeps 17 Dec at 2.75% as the base case for the ECB and a further BoE hike in play.","src":"Reuters via Global Banking & Finance; Newsquawk; TMGM","tag":"Policy","title":"Sleijpen: a December ECB hike is likely, the direction is clear, the path after is unclear; France’s finances must be fixed in Paris. Simkus: more tightening needed in 2027 if inflation persists. BoE’s Greene backs another rate rise; 50 bp would need clear evidence of energy costs feeding wages and prices.","url":"https://www.globalbankingandfinance.com/ecb-policy-direction-clear-december-uncertain-sleijpen/"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"A new trade risk for German exporters just as the Ifo improves; no rate or date confirmed.","src":"finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; Kotak Neo/Reuters","tag":"Trade","title":"Reports that Washington is preparing higher tariffs on imports from the EU, China and other countries over state subsidies (dpa-AFX); Reuters says new tariffs on 16 trading partners could be announced. DHL −3.1%, Kühne+Nagel −3.6%. S&P 500 −0.50% to 7,201.09, Nasdaq −1.48%, Dow +0.85% to 50,018.70, Nvidia −5.86%.","url":"https://www.finanzen.net/nachricht/aktien/roundup-aktien-europa-schluss-eurostoxx-schwach-rueckschlag-fuer-techwerte-16084557"},{"date":"2026-11-24","impact":"Gas stays above the €80 bad line, keeping the inflation score near its low.","src":"Trading Economics; Newsquawk; ANSA","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF +1.69% to €91.25 on cold into early December, weak wind and storage below 69%. Iran’s army chief Hatami orders Hormuz kept closed until Iran’s seven conditions are met; Qatar’s PM says Doha is working to revive US-Iran talks. Bund 3.26%, BTP-Bund 109.4 bp, EUR/USD +0.53% to 1.0961. FT: US delays about 60 Patriot interceptors for Ukraine.","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/eu-natural-gas"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"A relief rally inside the Risk-off: the STOXX 600 is 3 points short of the 640 restore line and the VIX closed 26.63, above 25.","src":"Trading Economics; Anadolu; Reuters via MarketScreener; Saxo; finanzen.net","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 +1.81% to 636.97, its best day since early May after five straight losses, as Nvidia reinstates Jensen Huang: technology +4.7% (ASML +8.92%, STMicro +8.99%, BE Semi +9.2%, AT&S +13%, Infineon +6.56%), Siemens Energy +6.88%, Deutsche Bank +4.79%, HSBC +3.2%; Nemetschek −5.7% on a JPMorgan downgrade. Euro Stoxx 50 +2.23% to 6,074.47, DAX +2.15% to 25,337.27, CAC 40 +1.48% to 8,044.84, FTSE 100 +1.30% to 10,480.82, IBEX +1.63% to 19,181.10, FTSE MIB +0.65% to 49,021.93.","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/stock-market"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The US leg of the Risk-off reversed on a governance decision, not on data; the VIX stayed above the 25 restore line.","src":"Saxo Asia Quick Take; Anadolu; KuCoin; Business Recorder (Reuters)","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 +4.73% to 7,237.17, its best day since April 2025 and the end of a seven-day fall; Nasdaq +6.51% to 24,089.56, Nasdaq-100 +6.9%, Dow +3.01% to 49,595.31; Nvidia +31.5%, a record $952B one-day gain, Philadelphia semis +14.03%; VIX −25.8% to 26.63; 2-year Treasury 4.696%, 10-year 4.948%; Brent −3.5% to $89.37, WTI −4.5% to $80.17; gold +2.7% to $3,634.58. Williams would not rule out consecutive hikes; Fed December about 98% priced (Saxo). US flash composite PMI 54.6 from 55.9.","url":"https://www.home.saxo/en-sg/content/articles/macro/asia-market-quick-take--24-november-2026-24112026"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The regime stays Slowdown: the composite sits between the 51 and 52 lines, with Germany and France pulling apart.","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext); ActionForex; Trading Economics","tag":"Data","title":"Euro area flash PMIs, November: composite 51.7 (unchanged, 51.9 poll), manufacturing 52.9 (52.8 poll, two-month high), services 51.2 (51.8 poll, five-month low); new orders up a fifth month, output prices weakest since August. Germany composite 55.0 (56-month high), France 46.2 (six-month low, Q4 recession risk). UK composite 52.3 from 52.9 (52.8 forecast). Williamson: near 0.3% q/q growth in Q4.","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"The ECB stays Tightening with December settled and 2027 open; the hawks are arguing about pace, not direction.","src":"Reuters via Investing.com; Trading Economics","tag":"Policy","title":"Simkus (Reuters, Vienna): not convinced a 25 bp December hike will be sufficient to fully control inflation; no major pressure to act quickly again after December, but further tightening might be needed in 2027 if pressures persist; plenty of early warning signs of second-round effects, wage tracker moving up slightly, low gas storage a risk. Reuters: markets see December as a done deal and one or two hikes in 2027, the first fully priced only by June; Trading Economics: two more 25 bp hikes by March 2027. Lane and Kocher also spoke.","url":"https://www.investing.com/news/economy-news/december-rate-hike-may-not-be-enough-but-not-in-a-rush-to-move-again-ecbs-simkus-5044712"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"Spreads tightened on the signal, but the rule is a January idea in a minority parliament; the 28 Nov Senate vote and 17 Dec limit still bind.","src":"Reuters via BusinessDay; AFP via The Local and Malay Mail; Trading Economics; OraFinanza via Investing.com","tag":"Politics","title":"Macron tells Le Figaro that if volatility persists into January a 2011-style constitutional golden rule on public finances could be worth exploring, with parliament consulted as soon as January; it needs a three-fifths majority in joint session, Le Pen is inclined to support a version of it, the left objects. France expects a 5.4% deficit in 2026 with debt near 120% of GDP. The OAT fell 8 bp to 4.79% and the OAT-Bund spread to about 151 bp (OraFinanza 150 bp); the BTP-Bund spread closed 106 bp.","url":"https://www.businessday.co.za/world/2026-11-23-macron-revives-push-to-enshrine-fiscal-rules-in-constitution/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"Gas and oil split: TTF near €90 keeps the inflation and ECB pressure, while oil at a three-month low eases it.","src":"Geagency; Scenari Economici; Notizie Geopolitiche; Trading Economics (Brent); TVG Daily Brief; Wisevoter","tag":"Energy","title":"TTF December spikes 8.6% at the open to €96.075, a three-week high, on cold forecasts (3 to 5°C below normal this week, 5 to 8°C below in early December), low wind, Norwegian outages, storage at 69.2% on 21 Nov and Qatar’s force majeure extended to end-December, then fades to about €89.8 (+1.6%). Brent −3.5% to $89.37 and WTI −4.5% to $80.17, three-month lows, on the IEA surplus and Kpler’s 9.5 mb/d Hormuz flows (10 days to 19 Nov, 12.2 mb/d in October). Tasnim denies the Kharg convoy plan; Trump (Fox, 22 Nov) says tankers testing the blockade would be at the bottom of the sea and he is inclined to resume strikes after the New Year; Vance talks with Qatar’s PM; UKMTO reports a tanker incident near Chabahar; Houthis claim an attack on the Rabigh refinery.","url":"https://geagency.it/breaking-news/gas-prezzo-ttf-amsterdam-torna-sotto-90-euro-mwh-16/"},{"date":"2026-11-23","impact":"Ukraine talks and a likely PP-Vox majority in Spain are next week’s political inputs; neither has moved spreads yet.","src":"Reuters via Business Recorder; Anadolu (19 Nov); Ara; To Vima","tag":"Geopolitics","title":"The White House says Trump meets NATO’s Rutte in Palm Beach late on 25 Nov after the Shenzhen meetings with Putin and Xi; Europe presses to be included in US-backed Ukraine talks after Merz said a Yalta-style format will not bring peace and talks should run as Ukraine, Russia, Europe and the US. Spain votes 29 Nov: 40dB (20 Nov) PP 31.9%, PSOE 31.2%, Vox 17.1%, PP plus Vox 189 seats; Sigma Dos 182 to 193; the CIS alone has the right at 155 to 171, short of 176; Sánchez has approved more than €1.5B of welfare decrees since parliament was dissolved.","url":"https://www.brecorder.com/news/40450481/trump-to-meet-nato-chief-mark-rutte-on-wednesday-white-house-says"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"The equity rule bound: the posture moves to Risk-off while sovereign spreads stay calm, so it is an imported AI correction inside a Slowdown.","src":"Capp Notes; dpa-AFX via onvista and MarketScreener; Reuters via CEO Economy; Share Talk; Yahoo Finance (DPA)","tag":"Markets","title":"STOXX 600 −2.00% to 625.65, a fifth straight loss, below the 635 Risk-off trigger and its worst week since March (about −5.8%); technology at its lowest since May (ASML −9.3%, STMicro −10.8%, Infineon −7.9%, Siemens Energy −6.6%), Vallourec −12.8%, Ubisoft −11.6%, Scout24 −8.0%, Thyssenkrupp −8%; only food and beverage rose (Nestlé +1.3%). Euro Stoxx 50 −2.81% to 5,941.95 (May low, week −7%), DAX −2.60% to 24,802.78 (below 25,000 and its 200-day average, week −7.28%), CAC 40 −1.89% to 7,927.75, FTSE 100 −1.02% to 10,346.34 (week −5.1%), SMI −1.02% to 14,572.14.","url":"https://cappnotes.substack.com/p/closing-look-friday-november-20-2026"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"The US confirmed Europe’s Risk-off after the close and the Monday open carries the gap; falling yields and gold say liquidation, not a growth shock yet.","src":"Capp Notes; The Wall Street Republic; StockTitan","tag":"Markets","title":"S&P 500 −3.70% to 6,910.39, a seventh straight loss, −8.6% on the week and a correction; Nasdaq −5.28% to 22,617.73, now down for 2026; Nasdaq-100 −5.61% and touched a bear market intraday; Dow −1.93% to 48,147.33; VIX 35.89, highest close since late March; 376 S&P decliners to 123. Nvidia −14.01% to $125.53 after Reuters reported SEC enforcement staff are examining potential accounting issues and California subpoenaed it over chips reaching China; AMD −13.25%, Micron −13.89%, Broadcom −12.19%, Intuit −12.77%, Tesla −7.6%; Anthropic −8.50% to $88.91 (low $83.05, IPO $83). 10-year Treasury −12 bp to 4.92%, 2-year 4.63%; gold −4.6% to about $3,515; Bitcoin below $60,000; TAIEX −6.23% (week −17.5%), Nikkei −5.06%.","url":"https://thewallstreetrepublic.substack.com/p/nvidia-crashes-1401-on-an-sec-probe"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"Confidence improved but stays weak and wages stay tame, while German pipeline prices argue the ECB has more to do; the regime stays Slowdown pending the 23 Nov PMIs.","src":"Newsquawk; fx.co; Poland Insight; Yahoo Finance (DPA); MT Newswires; Capp Notes","tag":"Data","title":"EC flash consumer confidence −16.9 in November from −17.9 (consensus −17.9). ECB negotiated wages +2.57% y/y in Q3 from a revised 2.45%. German producer prices +8.1% y/y in October (7.2% expected, 6.7% in September), the fastest since February 2023, +1.4% m/m, energy +18.7%. INSEE business climate 95 (97 expected), lowest since June, manufacturing 104. UK retail sales +0.7% m/m (flat expected, +2.3% y/y), GfK −22 from −24, October borrowing £17.0B against £18.0B expected. US UMich final sentiment 51.5, one-year inflation expectations 3.7% from 4.3%.","url":"https://www.newsquawk.com/headlines/european-consumer-confidence-flash-nov--169-vs-exp--179-prev--179"},{"date":"2026-11-20","impact":"December is unchanged on both sides of the Atlantic despite the rout; core yields fell on safe-haven demand, not on repricing.","src":"ECB (speech text); Unite.AI; Capp Notes; The Wall Street Republic; ANSA; Trading Economics","tag":"Policy","title":"Lagarde’s European Banking Congress keynote (Acting in time) proposes European public-private partnerships in which public money including the EU budget takes first losses on chip plants and data centres to draw in private capital, and warns against dependence on foreign providers; nothing on rates. Barchart: ECB 17 Dec hike 91%, Fed 9 Dec 90% to 91.5%, BoJ 18 Dec 79%. Fed’s Cook: waiting longer to hike risks getting behind the curve; Philadelphia’s Paulson open to back-to-back hikes; Trump threatens penalties on US firms building data centres abroad. Bund −11 bp to 3.30%, BTP spread 109.9 bp (open 103), OAT 4.87%, gilt 5.24%; Bund future +95 ticks.","url":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/press/key/date/2026/html/ecb.sp261120~e52bcc4db2.en.html"}],"headline":"The relief rally faded: the STOXX 600 fell 0.66% to 632.74 on 24 Nov, back under the 635 trigger, as chipmakers reversed (Aixtron −16.3%, STMicro −10.2%, Infineon −9.7%, ASML −5.6%) and miners fell almost 5%, while Sanofi rose 9.8% on its $41B Insmed deal; the Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.16% to 6,003.76, the DAX 1.31% to 25,005.51 and the FTSE 100 0.13% to 10,467.07. The Ifo rose to 90.6 (90.5 expected), the highest since May 2023, but Fuest said the recovery lacks breadth and manufacturing slipped. The Bund closed 3.26%, the BTP spread widened to 109 bp, EUR/USD rose 0.53% to 1.0961, TTF rose 1.7% to €91.25 as Iran’s army chief ordered Hormuz kept closed, and reports that Washington is preparing higher tariffs on the EU hit logistics stocks. The regime stays Slowdown, the posture Risk-off and the ECB Tightening, with Sleijpen calling a December hike likely.","marketClose":"Tue 24 Nov 2026","notes":["PMIs: euro area, German and French flash figures and polls are the Reuters text (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and the ActionForex table; the S&P Global release page, FXStreet, Investing Live and MLQ refused fetches. UK composite 52.3 (52.9 prior, 52.8 forecast) is the Trading Economics gilt page. US PMIs 54.6 and 54.7 are Saxo.","Calendar: the Ifo forecast (90.5 box, about 90.8 text) is Finance Calendar, which says no consensus has been published; the 25 Nov Italian auction sizes are OraFinanza via Investing.com. The Spanish polls are Ara (40dB 20 Nov, Sigma Dos 20 Nov, YouGov 15 Nov, CIS 12 Nov) and To Vima (PF Global 15 to 17 Nov).","Closes for 24 Nov: STOXX 600 632.74 (−0.66%) is the Reuters wire via MarketScreener, read from an extracted summary (Trading Economics news says 632, −0.7%). Euro Stoxx 50 6,003.76 is finanzen.net/dpa-AFX and goldesel; ad-hoc-news gives 6,003.10. DAX 25,005.51 is ad-hoc-news; goldesel gives the Xetra close as 25,017.81 (−1.26%). FTSE 100 10,467.07 is Share Talk and finanzen.net. CAC 40 8,040.49, IBEX 19,015.80 and FTSE MIB 48,121.19 are Trading Economics tables.","Bonds and FX for 24 Nov: Bund 3.26%, BTP 4.35% and the 109.4 bp spread are the ANSA close; Trading Economics shows Bund 3.2639, OAT 4.779 and gilt 5.1936 without a timestamp, so the OAT-Bund spread of about 151.5 bp is derived. EUR/USD 1.0961 is the Trading Economics dated close (Share Talk 1.0967). The VIX of 26.36 is a Yahoo snapshot, not a confirmed close.","TTF €91.25 is the Trading Economics 24 Nov value (previous 89.74, close to the ≈€89.8 Geagency level used for 23 Nov); no exchange settlement page was opened. No Brent settlement for 24 Nov was confirmed; sources conflicted between about $86 and $90.","Ifo: Newsquawk and QNC print expectations at 91.6, ActionForex and Trading Economics at 91.9; the row uses 91.9. October was revised to 90.1 from 90.2 in both documents. No ECB or BoE probability was printed on any page read this run.","Score changes this run: growth +1 to 46 on the Ifo; markets −2 to 28 on the STOXX 600 back under 635, the wider BTP spread and US tariff reports; inflation and policy unchanged; composite 33. No score moved 5 or more."],"posture":{"label":"Risk-off","note":"Stays Risk-off. The STOXX 600 fell 0.66% to 632.74 on 24 Nov, back under the 635 trigger and 7 points short of the 640 restore line, as technology fell nearly 3% and miners almost 5% a day after Monday’s 1.81% rebound; the Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.16%, the DAX 1.31% and the FTSE MIB 1.84%. In the US the S&P 500 fell 0.50% to 7,201.09 and the Nasdaq 1.48%, with Nvidia −5.86% and the semiconductor index −7.9%, while the Dow rose 0.85% to 50,018.70; the VIX was about 26.4 on a Yahoo snapshot, still above 25. Sovereigns stayed calm: the BTP-Bund spread 109 bp and the OAT-Bund spread about 151.5 bp, under the 150/180 bp stress lines that would make this a sovereign Risk-off. Neutral returns on a STOXX 600 close above 640 with the VIX under 25."},"regime":{"label":"Slowdown","note":"Stays Slowdown. The Ifo rose to 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 (90.5 expected), a seventh rise and the highest since May 2023, with current conditions 89.4 and expectations 91.9, which backs Germany’s 55.0 flash composite PMI; but manufacturing slipped to −1.2 from +0.9 and Fuest said the recovery lacks breadth and rests on deficit-financed public spending. The euro area flash composite PMI is 51.7, between the 51 line that would deepen the Slowdown and the 52 line that would reopen Expansion, with France at 46.2. Hard data remain weak: September industrial production −1.5% y/y, retail sales +0.4% y/y, unemployment 6.5%, Q3 GDP +0.4% q/q. EC sentiment on 27 Nov and the final PMIs on 1 and 3 Dec are next."},"schema":1,"sections":[{"id":"leading","rows":[{"change":"+0.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":48.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":49.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":50.8},{"l":"Mar 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":52.2},{"l":"May 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":51.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Sep 26","v":52.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":52.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":52.9}],"latest":"52.9","name":"HCOB Eurozone Manufacturing PMI","next":"Early Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"52.7 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash rose to 52.9 from 52.7, a two-month high and above the 52.8 Reuters poll, the tenth month above 50; the output index rose to 53.3 from 52.8. Total new orders across the economy rose for a fifth month at a slightly quicker pace and manufacturers’ order growth was stronger than services’, while new export orders grew at the slowest pace in three months; factory hiring was at a near three-and-a-half-year high. Input-cost inflation eased to a three-month low and selling prices rose at the weakest pace since August. Germany rose to 55.0 from 54.2 with output at 57.4, a 63-month high; France fell to 47.9 from 49.5 with output at 44.5, the weakest in 21 months. The final prints in early December.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, beat poll","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and ActionForex (23 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Two-month high","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"−0.2","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":52.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.2},{"l":"Apr 26","v":47.6},{"l":"May 26","v":47.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":49.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Aug 26","v":51.6},{"l":"Sep 26","v":53},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.4},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.2}],"latest":"51.2","name":"HCOB Eurozone Services PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"51.4 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash fell to 51.2 from 51.4, a five-month low and below the 51.8 Reuters poll that had looked for a rise. Services cut jobs for a second month, the year-ahead outlook fell for a fourth month to its lowest since May and output-price inflation slowed. Germany’s services PMI rose to 54.0 from 52.5; France’s rose to 46.6 from 46.2 but stayed in contraction with new business down markedly, and S&P Global’s Hayes said the French data raise the odds of a Q4 technical recession. The rest of the euro area grew at its slowest pace in six months; Finimize’s read of Williamson is that services feel high living costs and political uncertainty while investment orders hold up.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, missed poll","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com) and ActionForex (23 Nov)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Five-month low","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"0.0","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":51.5},{"l":"Jan 26","v":51.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":51.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":50.7},{"l":"Apr 26","v":48.8},{"l":"May 26","v":48.5},{"l":"Jun 26","v":50},{"l":"Jul 26","v":52},{"l":"Aug 26","v":52},{"l":"Sep 26","v":53.1},{"l":"Oct 26","v":51.7},{"l":"Nov 26","v":51.7}],"latest":"51.7","name":"HCOB Eurozone Composite PMI","next":"3 Dec (Nov final)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"51.7 (Oct final)","read":"The November flash composite held at 51.7, unchanged from October and below the 51.9 Reuters poll, a fifth straight month above 50; it sits between the 51 line that would deepen the Slowdown and the 52 line that would reopen Expansion. Manufacturing (52.9) carried the index while services (51.2) slipped. New orders rose for a fifth month and slightly faster, employment was broadly unchanged, input costs rose at a three-month low pace and output prices at the weakest since August. Germany’s composite rose to 55.0 from 53.4, a 56-month high; France fell to 46.2 from 46.6, its sharpest contraction in six months with sentiment near its worst since 2012 outside Covid. Williamson: steady growth consistent with a near 0.3% q/q pace in Q4, masking worrying divergences.","ref":50,"refLabel":"50 = expansion line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above 50, below 52","src":"S&P Global / HCOB via Reuters (RTE, Euronext, Investing.com), ActionForex and Trading Economics (23 Nov)","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Unchanged","url":"https://www.rte.ie/news/business/2026/1123/1600272-euro-zone-growth-pmi/"},{"change":"+1.0","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":-13.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-12.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-12.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-16.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-20.5},{"l":"May 26","v":-18.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-17.7},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-15.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-15.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-16.8},{"l":"Oct 26","v":-17.9},{"l":"Nov 26","v":-16.9}],"latest":"−16.9","name":"Euro Area Consumer Confidence","next":"27 Nov (final, with ESI)","period":"Nov 2026 flash","prior":"−17.9","read":"The November flash rose to −16.9 from −17.9 in October, one point better than the −17.9 consensus and the first improvement since August, but still below the −15 weak line of the framework and about six points under the long-run average of −11. The final October economic sentiment indicator was 96.9, a four-month low; the November ESI and the final confidence figure print on 27 Nov. Consumers remain the weak leg of the leading set, with German business surveys holding up and the French INSEE climate at a June low.","ref":-11,"refLabel":"−11 = long-run average","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Weak, below −15","src":"European Commission flash (20 Nov) via Newsquawk and fx.co; Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Improving","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/consumer-confidence"},{"change":"+7.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":45.8},{"l":"Jan 26","v":59.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":58.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-0.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":-17.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-10.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":10.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":26.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":34.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":34.7},{"l":"Oct 26","v":28.1},{"l":"Nov 26","v":35.6}],"latest":"35.6","name":"ZEW Economic Sentiment, Germany","next":"Mid-Dec","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"28.1","read":"Rose to 35.6 in November from 28.1, above the 28.3 consensus (Newsquawk 29) and recovering most of October’s fall. Current conditions improved to −33.2 from −35.9, in line with consensus, and euro area expectations rose to 26.5 from 18.5 against 18.3, with the euro area current index at −14.7. Wambach: the German economy remains on its recovery track at a slow rate, supported by rising manufacturing orders and lower oil prices, but government spending has been the main driver so far, which raises debt-sustainability concerns. One beat does not change the regime; the 23 Nov flash PMIs and 24 Nov Ifo follow.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = neutral","signal":"good","signalWord":"Above consensus","src":"ZEW via ActionForex and Newsquawk (17 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661152-germany-zew-sentiment-jumps-to-35-6-but-recovery-remains-uneven/"},{"change":"+0.5","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":87.6},{"l":"Jan 26","v":87.6},{"l":"Feb 26","v":88.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":86.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":84.4},{"l":"May 26","v":84.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":85.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":86.6},{"l":"Aug 26","v":88.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":89.9},{"l":"Oct 26","v":90.1},{"l":"Nov 26","v":90.6}],"latest":"90.6","name":"Ifo Business Climate, Germany","next":"Mid-Dec (December)","period":"Nov 2026","prior":"90.1 (Oct, rev. from 90.2)","read":"The Ifo rose to 90.6 in November from a revised 90.1 against 90.5 expected (Reuters poll), a seventh straight rise and the highest since May 2023. Current conditions rose to 89.4 from 88.8 and expectations to 91.9 from 91.5 (ActionForex and Trading Economics; Newsquawk and QNC print 91.6), slightly under the 92.0 consensus; services rose to +2.4 from −1.2 while manufacturing fell back to −1.2 from +0.9, its first setback after six gains, with expectations negative again. Fuest said the recovery is proceeding but lacks breadth and is mostly driven by deficit-financed public spending; the reading backs the 55.0 German flash PMI but not a broad-based upturn.","signal":"good","signalWord":"Highest since May 2023","src":"Ifo Institute via ActionForex (sector balances), Reuters via RTE (poll 90.5, Fuest), Trading Economics, Newsquawk","trend":"up","trendWord":"Seventh rise","url":"https://www.actionforex.com/live-comments/661933-germany-ifo-business-climate-rises-to-90-6-but-manufacturing-recovery-loses-momentum/"}],"sub":"Forward-looking. Read these first.","title":"Leading indicators"},{"id":"coincident","rows":[{"change":"−0.4 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":1.6},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":-1.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":-1.2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":-3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":0.2},{"l":"May 26","v":-0.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":-0.3},{"l":"Jul 26","v":-0.3},{"l":"Aug 26","v":-1.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":-1.5}],"latest":"−1.5%","name":"Industrial Production, y/y","next":"14 Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"−1.1%","read":"Rose 0.2% on the month in September, the first monthly gain in four months, but fell 1.5% on the year from −1.1% in August (August −0.5% m/m and −1.1% y/y, unrevised). Durable consumer goods rose 1.3% m/m and intermediate goods 0.7%, capital goods were flat, energy fell 1.7% and non-durable consumer goods 0.4%; the EU rose 0.5% m/m and fell 0.8% y/y, with Ireland, Bulgaria and Romania the largest annual falls and the Netherlands, Hungary, Lithuania and Malta the largest rises. The annual rate is the weakest of the year and sits against a manufacturing PMI of 52.7, so the coincident set still does not confirm the survey; ING’s Colijn said signs of a true rebound are not there. The Eurostat page redirected, so the figures are the Yahoo Finance and Fibre2Fashion reports of the release.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Fourth annual fall, m/m up","src":"Eurostat (4-16112026-AP, 16 Nov 2026) via Yahoo Finance and Fibre2Fashion","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/4-16112026-ap"},{"change":"−0.9 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.6},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1},{"l":"May 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":0.4},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":0.4}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"Retail Sales, y/y","next":"Early Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+1.3% (rev. from +0.8%)","read":"Volumes fell 0.7% on the month in September against a −0.1% consensus, the sharpest fall in over a year, and rose 0.4% on the year after August was revised up to +0.4% m/m and +1.3% y/y. Non-food fell 1.2% m/m, food 0.3% and fuel rose 1.0%; Germany fell 2.0% m/m and 2.4% y/y, France 0.6% m/m (+2.8% y/y), Spain 0.4% and Italy rose 0.1%. Consumers are cutting real spending as HICP runs at 4.0% and confidence sits at −17.9; the annual rate is still positive, so the signal is warn rather than bad.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Sharp monthly fall","src":"Eurostat (6 Nov 2026 release)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Falling","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/en/web/products-euro-indicators/w/4-06112026-ap"},{"change":"0.0 pt (+0.1 pt vs first print)","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Nov 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Dec 25","v":6.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Apr 26","v":6.4},{"l":"May 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6.5}],"latest":"6.5%","name":"Unemployment Rate","next":"Early Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"6.5% (rev. from 6.4%)","read":"September unemployment printed 6.5% against 6.4% expected, and the Eurostat series now shows July and August at 6.5% too (revised from 6.4%), so the rate has risen 0.2 pt from the 6.3% low of January. 11.555M people are unemployed, up 122K on the month, youth unemployment rose to 15.4% from 15.1%; Spain 10.3%, France 8.5%, Italy 6.3%, Germany 4.1%, Netherlands 4.0%; the EU rate is 6.2%. The level is still low in the five-year range but the direction has turned.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Rising from 6.3% low","src":"Eurostat API (une_rt_m, updated 30 Oct); Trading Economics","trend":"flat","trendWord":"Revised up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/unemployment-rate"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":-0.7},{"l":"Nov 25","v":10.5},{"l":"Dec 25","v":13},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Mar 26","v":6.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.6},{"l":"May 26","v":6.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6.5},{"l":"Jul 26","v":13.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.3}],"latest":"+3.3%","name":"German Factory Orders, y/y","next":"7 Dec (October)","period":"Sep 2026","prior":"+3.2% (rev. from +2.7%)","read":"Orders rose 4.4% on the month in September against a 4.8% consensus, recovering part of the August fall, which was revised to −10.1% from −10.6%. Orders are 3.3% above a year ago, and the August annual rate was revised to +3.2% from +2.7%. The rebound was broad: +5.1% excluding large orders, domestic +6.6%, foreign +2.8% (euro area +4.5%), autos +10.5% and machinery +8.3%, while other transport equipment fell 10.4%. Q3 orders were only 0.2% above Q2 and real manufacturing turnover rose 0.2% on the month, so the level is flat, not accelerating.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no change","signal":"good","signalWord":"Broad rebound","src":"Destatis (press release 385, 5 Nov 2026); Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound m/m","url":"https://www.destatis.de/DE/Presse/Pressemitteilungen/2026/11/PD26_385_421.html"},{"change":"−0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q4 23","v":0},{"l":"Q1 24","v":0.4},{"l":"Q2 24","v":0.2},{"l":"Q3 24","v":0.5},{"l":"Q4 24","v":0.3},{"l":"Q1 25","v":0.5},{"l":"Q2 25","v":0},{"l":"Q3 25","v":0.3},{"l":"Q4 25","v":0.2},{"l":"Q1 26","v":0},{"l":"Q2 26","v":0.6},{"l":"Q3 26","v":0.4}],"latest":"+0.4%","name":"Real GDP, q/q","next":"Early Dec (Q3 third estimate)","period":"Q3 2026 second estimate","prior":"+0.6%","read":"The second estimate on 13 Nov confirmed Q3 growth at +0.4% q/q (consensus 0.4%) and +1.3% y/y after 0.6% in Q2, with employment stable on the quarter: Ireland +5.5%, Spain +0.6%, Germany +0.2%, Italy 0.0%, France −0.1%; the EU grew 0.5%. Growth is positive, so the signal is good, but the composition is AI investment, defence and public spending with France and Italy flat to negative, the September goods balance swung to a €10.3B deficit on energy imports, and the ECB expects the yield rise to cost about 100 bp of growth over 2027-28. UK Q3 GDP rose 0.7% q/q the day before.","ref":0,"refLabel":"0 = no growth","signal":"good","signalWord":"Confirmed +0.4%","src":"Eurostat (2-13112026-AP, 13 Nov) via investingLive, Newsquawk and Eunews","trend":"down","trendWord":"Slower, beat","url":"https://ec.europa.eu/eurostat/web/products-euro-indicators/w/2-13112026-ap"}],"sub":"Confirmation of what the leading set signalled.","title":"Coincident indicators"},{"id":"policy","rows":[{"change":"+0.2 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.1},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.7},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.9},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.2},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.8},{"l":"Oct 26","v":4}],"latest":"4.0%","name":"HICP, y/y","next":"1 Dec (Nov flash)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"3.8%","read":"Eurostat confirmed October HICP at 4.0% y/y on 18 Nov, up from 3.8% and the highest since September 2023. Energy was 21.0% (19.0% in September; flash 20.8%), services 3.3% (3.2%), non-energy industrial goods 1.2% (1.1%) and food, alcohol and tobacco 1.5%, revised from 1.6% in the flash (1.3% in September). Lithuania printed 6.1%, Bulgaria 5.9%, Greece 5.7% and Italy 5.6%, up 1.4 pt on the month; Germany’s final was 2.9%. Pantheon projects 4.1% in November, 4.3% in December and a 4.5% peak in January, and the ECB expects inflation above 4% for much of the first half of 2027. The November flash is due 1 Dec. The Eurostat release page redirected to a maintenance notice; the figures are from Eunews and Trading Economics.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = ECB target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Confirmed, highest since Sep 2023","src":"Eurostat (2-18112026-AP, 18 Nov) via Eunews and Trading Economics","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.eunews.it/en/2026/11/18/eurozone-inflation-confirmed-at-4-per-cent-in-october/"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":2.4},{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.3},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.2},{"l":"May 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.4},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.6}],"latest":"2.6%","name":"Core HICP, y/y","next":"1 Dec (Nov flash)","period":"Oct 2026 final","prior":"2.5%","read":"Core HICP was revised up to 2.6% in the 18 Nov final from the 2.5% flash, a five-month high and the second monthly rise, with services at 3.3% (3.2% in September) and non-energy industrial goods 1.2% (1.1%). The energy shock is starting to show in underlying prices, though the ECB wage tracker of 4 Nov still has negotiated wages at 2.3% for 2026 and 2.8% for the first half of 2027. Lagarde said underlying inflation would take a particular role in the December decision; a core print above 2.6% in the 1 Dec flash strengthens the case for the market’s second 2027 hike. The Q3 negotiated wages print is 20 Nov.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = ECB target","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Five-month high","src":"Eurostat (18 Nov final) via Trading Economics; ECB wage tracker (4 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Revised up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/core-inflation-rate"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2},{"l":"May 26","v":2},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Aug 26","v":2.25},{"l":"Sep 26","v":2.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":2.5},{"l":"24 Nov","v":2.5}],"latest":"2.50%","name":"ECB Deposit Facility Rate","next":"16–17 Dec meeting (projection round)","period":"Since 16 Sep; held 29 Oct","prior":"2.25%","read":"The deposit rate is 2.50% after hikes in June and September and a hold on 29 Oct; a 25 bp hike to 2.75% on 17 Dec is the base case (Reuters: a done deal, with one or two more priced for 2027). Sleijpen said on 24 Nov that a December hike is likely, the direction is clear but the path after is unclear and the Council is meeting by meeting; Simkus (Newsquawk paraphrase) said further tightening will be needed in 2027 if inflation stays persistent. The Ifo at 90.6 and TTF at €91.25 support the hawks; the Council discusses doubling the minimum reserve ratio to 2% on 25 Nov and the European Council takes up the de Cos, Knot and Brunnermeier nominations on 26 Nov.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Tightening","src":"ECB; Reuters via Global Banking & Finance (Sleijpen, 24 Nov); Newsquawk (Simkus); Reuters (pricing, 23 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.ecb.europa.eu/stats/policy_and_exchange_rates/key_ecb_interest_rates/html/index.en.html"},{"change":"+0.12 pt","hist":[{"l":"Q3 25","v":1.89},{"l":"Q4 25","v":2.95},{"l":"Q1 26","v":2.56},{"l":"Q2 26","v":2.45},{"l":"Q3 26","v":2.57}],"latest":"2.57%","name":"Negotiated Wages, y/y","next":"Feb 2027 (Q4); tracker 18 Dec","period":"Q3 2026","prior":"2.45% (rev. from 2.44%)","read":"Negotiated wages rose 2.57% y/y in Q3 2026 from a revised 2.45% in Q2, a modest acceleration of 0.12 point and far below the 5.55% peak of 2024, so the 4.0% HICP print has not yet fed into pay settlements. The ECB wage tracker of 4 Nov shows 2.7% for the second half of 2026 and 2.8% for the first half of 2027 with smoothed one-offs, on 45.6% and 31.8% coverage; the September staff projection has compensation per employee at 3.3% for 2026. This remains the main argument that the energy shock is not turning into a wage-price loop, and Nagel said on 19 Nov he sees no spiral. The next tracker update is 18 Dec, after the ECB meeting.","ref":3,"refLabel":"3% = roughly consistent with 2% inflation","signal":"good","signalWord":"No wage spiral","src":"ECB indicator of negotiated wages (20 Nov) via Poland Insight; ECB wage tracker release of 4 Nov","trend":"up","trendWord":"Edging up","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/negotiated-wage-growth"},{"change":"+0.1 pt","hist":[{"l":"Nov 25","v":3.2},{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":2.8},{"l":"May 26","v":2.8},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.6},{"l":"Jul 26","v":2.9},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.5},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.6}],"latest":"3.6%","name":"UK CPI, y/y","next":"16 Dec (Nov)","period":"Oct 2026","prior":"3.5%","read":"Rose to 3.6% in October from 3.5%, a one-year high but below the 3.7% consensus and the BoE’s 3.7% projection. Core held at 2.8%, services eased to 3.7% (consensus 3.8%, BoE 3.9%), goods rose to 3.5% from 3.2% and CPIH to 3.7%; prices rose 0.5% on the month. Transport, mainly motor fuels, was the largest upward contribution with the earlier tobacco duty rise, restaurants and hotels the main offset; food rose 0.9%, the smallest since September 2021. The BoE projects nearly 5% in Q2 2027 on energy. December hike pricing fell to about 80% from 90% with three more hikes by June still priced; the 10-year gilt fell 9 bp to 5.30%. Next release 16 Dec.","ref":2,"refLabel":"2% = BoE target","signal":"bad","signalWord":"One-year high, below consensus","src":"ONS (18 Nov 2026 bulletin); Reuters via Investing.com; Trading Economics (gilts and pricing); Alliance News via BritBrief","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/inflationandpriceindices/bulletins/consumerpriceinflation/october2026"},{"change":"+25 bp","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":3.75},{"l":"Jan 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Feb 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3.75},{"l":"May 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Jun 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.75},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.75},{"l":"24 Nov","v":4}],"latest":"4.00%","name":"BoE Bank Rate","next":"17 Dec","period":"Since 5 Nov 2026","prior":"3.75%","read":"Bank Rate is 4.00% after the 5 Nov hike, the first since 2023, by 8 votes to 1. October CPI of 3.6% (3.7% expected) cut December pricing to about 80%, and the 20 Nov data were firm: retail sales rose 0.7% m/m (flat expected, ex-fuel +0.9%, +2.3% y/y), GfK confidence improved to −22 from −24 and October borrowing was £17.0B against £18.0B expected and a revised £22.9B in September. Trading Economics says markets price at least three more hikes by the end of 2027. The 10-year gilt fell below 5.3% to 5.24% on global risk aversion as the FTSE 100 lost 1.02% and 5.1% on the week; sterling ended about 1.31. The UK-EU Brussels summit agreed to link emissions trading systems, reduce agri-food checks from 2028 and start an economic-security dialogue. Greene told a SIGNUM roundtable on 24 Nov that she backs another rate increase, that disinflation has stalled and that a 50 bp move would need clear evidence of energy costs passing into wages and prices (TMGM summary).","signal":"warn","signalWord":"First hike since 2023","src":"Bank of England (Monetary Policy Summary, 5 Nov 2026); ONS retail sales and public finances via MT Newswires and Share Talk (20 Nov); Trading Economics gilt news (20 Nov)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Hiking","url":"https://www.bankofengland.co.uk/monetary-policy-summary-and-minutes/2026/november-2026"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the leading and coincident core. They are not part of the original indicator list.","title":"Inflation and policy"},{"id":"markets","rows":[{"change":"−2 bp (TE 3.2639)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":2.81},{"l":"Jan 26","v":2.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":2.75},{"l":"Mar 26","v":2.91},{"l":"Apr 26","v":3},{"l":"May 26","v":3.05},{"l":"Jun 26","v":2.96},{"l":"Jul 26","v":3.07},{"l":"Aug 26","v":3.18},{"l":"Sep 26","v":3.58},{"l":"Oct 26","v":3.32},{"l":"24 Nov","v":3.26}],"latest":"3.26%","name":"10-Year Bund Yield","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"3.28% on 23 Nov","read":"The Bund closed 3.26% on 24 Nov per ANSA, from 3.28%, with Newsquawk at 3.266% mid-morning and Trading Economics at 3.2639; the Bund future traded 122.80 to 123.09. Bonds held their bid as equities gave back Monday’s rally and the US 10-year eased to about 4.90%. Sleijpen said a December hike is likely but the path after is unclear, which keeps the front end anchored near 2.75% and the 10-year below its September high of 3.58%.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Above 3%","src":"ANSA (close 3.26%); Newsquawk (3.266%); Trading Economics (3.2639)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Edging lower","url":"https://www.ansa.it/sito/notizie/topnews/2026/11/24/lo-spread-btp-bund-chiude-a-109-punti_5168cfc5-e9c4-4dee-abc5-0d917d01948d.html"},{"change":"+3 bp (opened 105 bp)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":74},{"l":"Jan 26","v":68},{"l":"Feb 26","v":64},{"l":"Mar 26","v":82},{"l":"Apr 26","v":82},{"l":"May 26","v":79},{"l":"Jun 26","v":77},{"l":"Jul 26","v":81},{"l":"Aug 26","v":81},{"l":"Sep 26","v":106},{"l":"Oct 26","v":118},{"l":"24 Nov","v":109.4}],"latest":"109 bp","name":"BTP–Bund 10-Year Spread","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"106 bp on 23 Nov","read":"The spread closed 109.4 bp on 24 Nov per ANSA after opening at 105 bp, with the BTP up to 4.35% and the Bund at 3.26%, as the FTSE MIB fell 1.84% and banks declined. It stays well under the 150 bp warn line. The OAT-Bund spread is about 151.5 bp on the Trading Economics yields (OAT 4.779%), unchanged and still at the warn line; Sleijpen said France’s public finances must be fixed in Paris primarily. Italy sells BTP Short Term and BTP€i on 25 Nov, BOTs on 26 Nov and medium and long BTPs on 27 Nov.","ref":150,"refLabel":"150 bp = warn line","signal":"good","signalWord":"Under 150 bp","src":"ANSA (open 105 bp, close 109.4 bp, BTP 4.35%, Bund 3.26%); Trading Economics (OAT 4.779%); Reuters via Global Banking & Finance (Sleijpen)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Wider","url":"https://www.ansa.it/sito/notizie/topnews/2026/11/24/lo-spread-btp-bund-chiude-a-109-punti_5168cfc5-e9c4-4dee-abc5-0d917d01948d.html"},{"change":"−0.66% on 24 Nov","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":592.19},{"l":"Jan 26","v":611},{"l":"Feb 26","v":633.85},{"l":"Mar 26","v":583.14},{"l":"Apr 26","v":611.28},{"l":"May 26","v":626},{"l":"Jun 26","v":641.73},{"l":"Jul 26","v":649.19},{"l":"Aug 26","v":651.1},{"l":"Sep 26","v":634.89},{"l":"Oct 26","v":646.75},{"l":"24 Nov","v":632.74}],"latest":"632.74","name":"STOXX Europe 600","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"636.97 on 23 Nov","read":"The STOXX 600 fell 0.66% to 632.74 on 24 Nov (Reuters via MarketScreener; Trading Economics 632, −0.7%) as Monday’s tech rally faded: technology fell nearly 3% (Aixtron −16.3% on a UBS sell call, STMicro −10.2%, Infineon −9.7%, ASM International more than −8%, ASML −5.6%), miners almost 5% (Aurubis −15%, Antofagasta −8.1%), Siemens Energy −6.8% and Schneider −4%. Health care rose nearly 2% as Sanofi gained 9.8%, its best day since 2008, on its $41B purchase of Insmed; food and beverage and household goods rose about 1%, Compass +4.2%. DHL and Kühne+Nagel fell more than 3% on reports that Washington is preparing higher tariffs on the EU, China and others. The index is back under the 635 Risk-off trigger, 4.7% below the 13 Nov record of 664.02 and 7 points short of the 640 restore line.","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Below 635 trigger","src":"Reuters via MarketScreener (632.74, −0.66%, sectors); Trading Economics; finanzen.net/dpa-AFX (stocks, tariffs); goldesel.de","trend":"down","trendWord":"Rally faded","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/european-shares-end-lower-as-tech-rally-fades-sanofi-gains-ce7851dbdd89f025"},{"change":"−1.16% on 24 Nov (−70.71)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":5791.41},{"l":"Jan 26","v":5947.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":6138.41},{"l":"Mar 26","v":5569.73},{"l":"Apr 26","v":5881.51},{"l":"May 26","v":6050.54},{"l":"Jun 26","v":6328.09},{"l":"Jul 26","v":6358.01},{"l":"Aug 26","v":6420.16},{"l":"Sep 26","v":6269.02},{"l":"Oct 26","v":6131.31},{"l":"24 Nov","v":6003.76}],"latest":"6,003.76","name":"Euro Stoxx 50","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"6,074.47 on 23 Nov","read":"The Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.16% to 6,003.76 on 24 Nov (finanzen.net/dpa-AFX; ad-hoc-news 6,003.10, −1.17%), giving back half of Monday’s 2.23% gain as chip names reversed (Infineon −9.7%, STMicro −10.2%, ASML −5.6%) and Siemens and Schneider fell about 4%; Sanofi’s 9.8% rise was the main offset. The index sits just above the 20 Nov May low of 5,941.95 and 2.1% below its October close of 6,131.31.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Near May low","src":"finanzen.net/dpa-AFX (6,003.76, −70.71); ad-hoc-news; Trading Economics","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://www.finanzen.net/nachricht/aktien/roundup-aktien-europa-schluss-eurostoxx-schwach-rueckschlag-fuer-techwerte-16084557"},{"change":"−1.31% on 24 Nov (−331.76)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":24490.41},{"l":"Jan 26","v":24538.81},{"l":"Feb 26","v":25284.26},{"l":"Mar 26","v":22680.04},{"l":"Apr 26","v":24292.38},{"l":"May 26","v":25104.7},{"l":"Jun 26","v":24995.81},{"l":"Jul 26","v":25629.24},{"l":"Aug 26","v":26258.11},{"l":"Sep 26","v":25199.19},{"l":"Oct 26","v":25818.21},{"l":"24 Nov","v":25005.51}],"latest":"25,005.51","name":"DAX","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"25,337.27 on 23 Nov","read":"The DAX fell 1.31% to 25,005.51 on 24 Nov (ad-hoc-news; goldesel gives the Xetra close as 25,017.81, −1.26%), holding just above 25,000 despite an Ifo at 90.6, the highest since May 2023, as Infineon fell 9.7%, Aixtron 16.3%, Siemens Energy 6.8% and Hochtief 5.4%, and logistics names fell on US tariff reports; Deutsche Telekom, Beiersdorf and Deutsche Börse rose about 2%. The MDAX fell 1.72% to 28,791.63. The index is 3.2% below its October close of 25,818.21.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Just above 25,000","src":"ad-hoc-news (25,005.51, −331.76); goldesel.de (Xetra 25,017.81, MDAX); finanzen.net/dpa-AFX","trend":"down","trendWord":"Lower","url":"https://www.ad-hoc-news.de/boerse/news/marktberichte/dax-closes-1-31-prozent-lower-as-tech-rally-loses-momentum/70550649"},{"change":"−0.13% on 24 Nov (−13.75 pt)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":9931.4},{"l":"Jan 26","v":10223.5},{"l":"Feb 26","v":10910.6},{"l":"Mar 26","v":10176.5},{"l":"Apr 26","v":10378.8},{"l":"May 26","v":10409.3},{"l":"Jun 26","v":10497.1},{"l":"Jul 26","v":10868.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":10824.3},{"l":"Sep 26","v":10606},{"l":"Oct 26","v":10729.78},{"l":"24 Nov","v":10467.07}],"latest":"10,467.07","name":"FTSE 100","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"10,480.82 on 23 Nov","read":"The FTSE 100 slipped 0.13% to 10,467.07 on 24 Nov (Share Talk; finanzen.net) as miners fell with copper down 5.6% and silver 5.1% (Antofagasta −8.1%, Anglo American −5.7%, Fresnillo −4.9%, Glencore −4.8%), offset by defensives and data names: Experian +4.6%, Compass +4.2% on full-year results and a planned US primary listing, RELX +3.1%, LSEG +2.4%, AstraZeneca and GSK higher. The FTSE 250 fell 0.8% to 23,858.88. The gilt eased to about 5.19% and sterling rose to $1.3110; BoE’s Greene backed another rate rise.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Below Oct close","src":"Share Talk (10,467.07, movers, FTSE 250, GBP/USD); finanzen.net; Trading Economics (gilt 5.1936); TMGM (Greene)","trend":"down","trendWord":"Flat to lower","url":"https://www.share-talk.com/ftse-100-closes-lower-as-nvidia-falls-8-and-mining-stocks-tumble/"},{"change":"+0.53% on 24 Nov (TE +0.0058)","hist":[{"l":"Dec 25","v":1.1747},{"l":"Jan 26","v":1.1855},{"l":"Feb 26","v":1.1803},{"l":"Mar 26","v":1.146},{"l":"Apr 26","v":1.1685},{"l":"May 26","v":1.166},{"l":"Jun 26","v":1.1422},{"l":"Jul 26","v":1.1524},{"l":"Aug 26","v":1.1589},{"l":"Sep 26","v":1.1341},{"l":"Oct 26","v":1.1184},{"l":"24 Nov","v":1.0961}],"latest":"1.0961","name":"EUR/USD","period":"24 Nov close","prior":"1.0903 on 23 Nov","read":"EUR/USD rose 0.53% to 1.0961 on 24 Nov on the Trading Economics close (Share Talk 1.0967), recovering from Monday’s 1.0903, the weakest since March 2025, as the Ifo beat backed ECB hike pricing and the dollar index fell about 0.5% to 103.82 even after US Conference Board confidence jumped to 103.3 from 91.9 (92.2 expected). The pair is still 2.0% below its October close of 1.1184; a weaker euro adds to imported energy inflation.","signal":"warn","signalWord":"Under 1.10","src":"Trading Economics (1.0961, +0.53%); Share Talk (1.0967); Kotak Neo/Reuters (DXY 103.82, Conference Board)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rebound","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/euro-area/currency"},{"change":"+1.69% on 24 Nov (+1.51)","hist":[{"l":"Oct 25","v":31.1},{"l":"Nov 25","v":28.8},{"l":"Dec 25","v":28.2},{"l":"Jan 26","v":39.3},{"l":"Apr 26","v":46},{"l":"May 26","v":46},{"l":"Jun 26","v":43.4},{"l":"Jul 26","v":59.1},{"l":"Aug 26","v":69.8},{"l":"Sep 26","v":72.4},{"l":"Oct 26","v":103.94},{"l":"24 Nov","v":91.25}],"latest":"€91.25","name":"TTF Natural Gas","period":"24 Nov (TE close)","prior":"€89.74 on 23 Nov (TE previous)","read":"TTF front-month rose 1.69% to €91.25 per MWh on 24 Nov (Trading Economics), trading €88.21 to €91.75 by mid-morning per Newsquawk after Monday’s spike to €96.08, on cold forecasts persisting into early December, weaker wind mid-week, storage below 69% and constrained Gulf LNG; ANSA reported futures at €92.3 late in the session. Iran’s army chief Hatami ordered the Strait of Hormuz to stay closed until Iran’s seven conditions are met (IRIB via Newsquawk) while Qatar’s prime minister said Doha is working to revive US-Iran talks; Kpler puts about 12 mtpa of Qatari LNG capacity offline through 2027. No exchange settlement page was opened. The price is about €11 above the €80 bad line.","ref":50,"refLabel":"€50 = warn, €80 = bad","signal":"bad","signalWord":"Above €80 bad line","src":"Trading Economics (91.25, previous 89.74); Newsquawk (range, Hormuz, Kpler); ANSA headline (€92.3)","trend":"up","trendWord":"Higher","url":"https://tradingeconomics.com/commodity/eu-natural-gas"},{"change":"n/a","hist":[],"latest":"≈300 bp","name":"iTraxx Crossover","period":"28 Sep 2026, intraday","prior":"About 315–320 bp in mid-May 2026","read":"No dated level was found for 17 to 23 Nov. The last confirmed print is about 300 bp touched intraday on 28 Sept 2026 (Reuters, S&P Global data), below the 315 to 320 bp range of mid-May. The row stays n/a until a dated page gives a level. The 23 Nov relief rally (STOXX 600 +1.81%, S&P 500 +4.73%, VIX down to 26.63) argues for a tighter print than Friday, while the BTP spread at 106 bp and the OAT-Bund spread near 151 bp say the sovereign market is calm.","signal":"neutral","signalWord":"Stale, 28 Sep","src":"Reuters via Gate (28 Sep 2026); cbonds","trend":"up","trendWord":"Rising","url":"https://www.gate.com/en-us/news/detail/itraxx-european-crossover-index-touches-300-basis-points-for-first-time-17884358"}],"sub":"Tracker rows added alongside the core. Closing levels for Thursday 19 Nov unless a date is shown.","title":"Market readings"}],"takeaways":["Monday’s bounce did not hold. The STOXX 600 fell 0.66% to 632.74 on 24 Nov (Reuters via MarketScreener), back under the 635 trigger and 4.7% below the 13 Nov record; technology fell nearly 3% (Aixtron −16.3% on a UBS sell call, STMicro −10.2%, Infineon −9.7%, ASM International more than −8%, ASML −5.6%), miners almost 5% (Aurubis −15%, Antofagasta −8.1%), Siemens Energy −6.8%. Health care rose nearly 2% as Sanofi gained 9.8% on its $41B Insmed purchase, its best day since 2008. The Euro Stoxx 50 fell 1.16% to 6,003.76, the DAX 1.31% to 25,005.51, the CAC 40 0.05% to 8,040.49, the FTSE 100 0.13% to 10,467.07, the IBEX 0.86% to 19,015.80 and the FTSE MIB 1.84% to 48,121.19.","The Ifo confirmed German momentum but not breadth. The business climate rose to 90.6 from a revised 90.1 (90.5 expected), a seventh rise and the highest since May 2023; current conditions rose to 89.4 from 88.8 and expectations to 91.9 from 91.5, a touch under 92.0 expected. Services rose to +2.4 from −1.2 while manufacturing fell to −1.2 from +0.9 after six gains; Fuest said the recovery is proceeding but lacks breadth and mostly reflects deficit-financed public spending.","The US split along the AI line. The S&P 500 fell 0.50% to 7,201.09 and the Nasdaq 1.48% to 23,733.54, with Nvidia −5.86% to $155.38, Analog Devices about −17% and the semiconductor index −7.9%, while the Dow rose 0.85% to 50,018.70 and health care 2.88%. Conference Board confidence jumped to 103.3 from 91.9 against 92.2 expected, the highest since January 2025, and the dollar index still fell about 0.5% to 103.82. Reuters reported the administration could announce new tariffs on 16 trading partners, and dpa-AFX that higher tariffs on the EU and China are being prepared over subsidies; DHL and Kühne+Nagel fell more than 3%.","Bonds and the euro held up. The Bund closed 3.26% from 3.28% (ANSA), the BTP 4.35% with the spread at 109.4 bp from 106 bp, the OAT 4.779% for an OAT-Bund spread of about 151.5 bp, and the gilt about 5.19%. EUR/USD rose 0.53% to 1.0961 after Monday’s 1.0903 low. Sleijpen said a December hike is likely and the path after unclear, and that France’s finances must be fixed in Paris; BoE’s Greene backed another UK rise.","Gas stayed high. TTF rose 1.69% to €91.25 on cold forecasts into early December, weak wind and storage below 69%, after Monday’s €96.08 spike; Iran’s army chief Hatami ordered Hormuz kept closed until Iran’s seven conditions are met, while Qatar’s prime minister said Doha is working to revive US-Iran talks. Brent traded $85.63 to $87.13 for February in the European morning (Newsquawk); no settlement was confirmed. The FT reported the US is delaying about 60 Patriot interceptors for Ukraine. Spain votes 29 Nov and the French Senate votes the revenue part of the budget on 28 Nov."],"translation":[{"body":"The STOXX 600 fell 0.66% to 632.74 on 24 Nov, back under the 635 trigger, so Monday’s 1.81% rebound reads as short-covering: the chip names that led it (Aixtron, STMicro, Infineon, ASML) gave back 6% to 16% and miners fell almost 5%. Under the framework the posture stays Risk-off until a close above 640 with the VIX under 25; the VIX is about 26.4. Leadership rotated to defensives, with health care up nearly 2% on Sanofi’s 9.8% gain and food, household goods and telecoms higher. The Ifo at 90.6 supports German domestic and services earnings but not manufacturing, and US tariff reports weigh on logistics and exporters; the Euro Stoxx 50 at 6,003.76 sits 1% above its May low.","title":"Equities"},{"body":"Core bonds held their bid: the Bund closed 3.26%, the gilt about 5.19% and the US 10-year about 4.90%. The periphery widened slightly with equities: the BTP-Bund spread closed 109.4 bp from 106 bp, still well under 150 bp, and the OAT-Bund spread held about 151.5 bp at the warn line ahead of the Senate’s 28 Nov revenue vote. EUR/USD recovered 0.53% to 1.0961 as the Ifo backed ECB pricing and the dollar index fell to 103.82, despite a strong US confidence print. Italian auctions on 25 to 27 Nov test periphery demand.","title":"Fixed income and currency"},{"body":"Policy is tightening into a two-speed economy: Sleijpen calls a December ECB hike to 2.75% likely with the path after unclear, Simkus sees 2027 tightening if inflation persists, and the Council discusses doubling the minimum reserve ratio on 25 Nov. The Ifo at 90.6 and TTF at €91.25 strengthen the hawks; France at 46.2 on the PMI and negotiated wages at 2.57% argue December could be the last. The BoE is at 4.00% with Greene backing another rise, and a Fed hike on 9 Dec is priced. Liquidity for European risk improves only if the VIX falls under 25 and TTF back under €80; neither holds now.","title":"Liquidity and policy"}]};
